# Chapter 4 - Intune's Capabilities in Analytics and Automation
# From the book "AI, Automation and Analytics - Transforming Device Management
# with Intune" (2nd Edition) by Jannik Reinhard.
# https://github.com/JayRHa/intune-aaa-book-code
#
# The Intune admin center is a website built on top of Microsoft Graph. Anything
# the portal does, you can do yourself. These are the read-only Graph calls from
# the chapter. Run them in Graph Explorer (aka.ms/ge) signed in with an account
# that holds the matching DeviceManagement*.Read.All scopes, or paste an Entra
# bearer token into the Authorization header below and use any HTTP client / the
# VS Code REST Client extension.
#
# A Graph request is:  https://graph.microsoft.com/{version}/{resource}?{query}
#   version:  v1.0 for production, beta for preview functionality
#   verbs:    GET reads, POST creates, PATCH updates, PUT replaces, DELETE removes

@graph = https://graph.microsoft.com
# @token = <paste an Entra bearer token here if not using Graph Explorer>

### Who am I
GET {{graph}}/v1.0/me
# Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### First ten users
GET {{graph}}/v1.0/users?$top=10

### Managed devices, only the columns we care about
GET {{graph}}/v1.0/deviceManagement/managedDevices?$select=deviceName,complianceState,operatingSystem

### Tenant device overview (the number the Intune home page shows)
GET {{graph}}/v1.0/deviceManagement/managedDeviceOverview

### Reading collected inventory through Graph (currently beta only)
### Replace DEVICE_ID with a real managedDevice id.
GET {{graph}}/beta/deviceManagement/managedDevices('DEVICE_ID')/deviceInventories

### One inventory topic, expanded to its individual properties
GET {{graph}}/beta/deviceManagement/managedDevices('DEVICE_ID')/deviceInventories('Battery')?$expand=instances($expand=microsoft.graph.deviceInventorySimpleItem/properties)
